Add explicit Generate requests and Generated responses using factory-supplied executable transform bindings

Dev accepted Generate -> Generated, then factory-supplied executable transform bindings
in the 2026-10-15 Codex session after the chapter-94 handoff at a3d2d35. User.Generate
names a held domain and transform by TypeName. Editor.Generated derives User.Response,
carrying those names, Emissions and inherited Problems through the existing consumer.
Generate proposes the accepted ontology unchanged; acceptance never triggers generation.

TransformBinding is a definition-backed, codec-less capability supplied as the optional
fourth Editor factory parameter (empty by default). It contains the transform domain,
its super-domain snapshots and pairs of concrete rule definition/executable RuleType.
RuleType reports the generic Rule definition, so the association is explicitly supplied
by the trusted caller; no reflection, compilation or activation is inferred. Matching
checks all held domain/member snapshots, super-domain coverage and the complete rule
set, plus a TypeDefinition source and named target. The first binding is GenDrake:
Emit and inherited EmissionReceived, exactly the GenDrakeTest chain. GenScala is not
migrated. Bindings are executable capabilities, not serialized user requests.

GenerateRequested is the fifth Editor rule. It refuses unheld names, absent/duplicate
bindings and snapshot mismatches by Problems, and queues a response function. The
actor invokes it after validation has collected all Problems and closed its session;
only a quiet result executes the binding in a fresh default-mode session. The input
is the domain definition plus held members. Emission count must equal definition count;
execution exceptions/count mismatch produce a refused Generated with no partial output,
and finally closes the session. Repeated requests start fresh. No ontology changes or
deliveries, file writes or activation. The actor gains no case dispatch. User now holds
eight members and Editor nine; ten patterns build the Editor validation knowledge.

The Generated codec exposed a transitive eligibility defect: namesCodecless inspected
Emission before target normalization, mistaking neutral Text for a codec-less type even
though normal Emission generation spells it String and emits a codec. Apply targetTypes
to the referenced definition and parent context before hasCodec, matching generate's
entry contract. The corpus gates pass without projection changes. A core legacy type
also uses the name Generated; fully qualified references in these new definitions avoid
its wildcard import shadowing the Editor response.

